.TH CPMDW 1 2026-11-24 "CP/M Disk Wrangler" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
cpmdw \- list, extract, add and change files on CP/M disc images
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cpmdw
.RI [ "global options" ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.IR arguments ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cpmdw
is the console version of CP/M Disk Wrangler: the same detection, the same checks and the same checked save as the
window, with no display needed, so it works in scripts.
It reads and writes CP/M discs in raw sector images, ImageDisk
.RI ( .imd ),
CPCEMU DSK and extended DSK files, and reads TeleDisk
.RI ( .td0 )
files.
It also reads and writes Torch CP/N discs, whose CP/M files are kept in Torch's own filesystem, and Research Machines
double-sided discs, which hold a separate CP/M disc on each side.
.PP
Global options may come before or after the command.
An image and a path on it are always separate arguments
.RB ( "cpmdw get games.img 0:GAME.COM out/" ),
never
.IR image : path .
.PP
Each run opens the image, makes its changes as one transaction and saves once: the directory is checked first, the new
file is written beside the old one and read back, then put in its place.
If anything fails, the image is left exactly as it was.
.SS Paths
A file is named
.IB user : NAME.EXT
or just
.I NAME.EXT
(user 0).
.B 3:
is user area 3,
.B *:FILE.TXT
is that name in every user area,
.B *:
is every user area, and the root
.RB ( /
or nothing) is the whole disc.
The wildcards
.B *
and
.B ?\&
expand in
.BR get ,
.BR rm ,
.B attr
and
.BR ls .
.SS Formats
CP/M discs don't say how they are laid out, so
.B cpmdw
works the format out from the disc (its directory, the files on it, the image's size and sectors), using a library of
formats: cpmtools' diskdefs and the program's own.
.B "cpmdw new \-\-list"
lists them.
.PP
When it can't be sure, because the directory is empty (a disc just made with
.BR new ,
for one), or the layout was found only by searching, the disc opens read-only, and every command that changes it needs
.BI \-\-format= NAME
to say which format it is; without it the change fails with exit status 6.
.B info
says which format was found and how sure it is.
Once a disc has files on it, detection usually confirms its format on its own.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON document on standard output (see
.BR "JSON OUTPUT" )
and nothing else there.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Do everything, including every check, but write nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-vol= NAME
Which volume of the image:
.B cpm
for a CP/M disc,
.B side0
and
.B side1
for a disc with a separate CP/M disc on each side,
.B torch
for a Torch CP/N disc.
.B all
lists every volume, for
.B ls
and
.BR info .
.TP
.BI \-\-fs= ID
Read the image as this filesystem, whatever detection says
.RB ( info
lists the readings and their ids).
.TP
.B \-\-backup
Keep the old file as
.IB name .bak
when saving.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
No output on success.
.TP
.BR \-f " \fINAME\fR, " \-\-format= \fINAME\fR
The disc's format, one of the names
.B new \-\-list
gives; on
.BR new ,
the format to make.
.TP
.BI \-\-diskdefs= FILE
Read more formats from a diskdefs file (cpmtools' syntax); may be given more than once.
A format from such a file is known only in runs that name the file.
.TP
.BI \-\-charset= NAME
The national character set for names and text:
.B ascii
(as on the disc),
.BR dk\-no ,
.BR se\-fi ,
.BR de ,
.BR fr ,
.BR it ,
.B es
or
.BR pt .
The disc always holds the 7-bit bytes; the set changes only how names are shown and how host names are written.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the commands and global options;
.IB command " \-\-help"
prints that command's own options.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and the DiskWranglerCore version.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS info
.B cpmdw info
.I image
.PP
What the image is: the container, the detection result and the other readings, the format and how sure it is, and
each volume with its free space.
For a layout in no library, its definition in diskdefs syntax, to save to a file for
.BR \-\-diskdefs .
.SS check
.B cpmdw check
.I image
.RB [ \-\-strict ]
.PP
Check the directory of every volume.
Exit status 4 if one fails;
.B \-\-strict
counts warnings as failures too.
.SS ls
.B cpmdw ls
.I image
.RI [ path ]
.RB [ \-R ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.PP
List the files, by user number, then name: name, attributes
.RB ( R
read-only,
.B S
system,
.B A
archive), size, and the date on a CP/M 3 disc with time stamps.
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recursive
lists every level below the path;
.BR \-d ", " \-\-directory
shows the path itself.
.SS get
.B cpmdw get
.I image path... hostdir
.PP
Extract files into the host folder
.IR hostdir ,
or, with
.B \-
as
.IR hostdir ,
write one file to standard output.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-user\-folders= auto | on | off
Put each user area's files in a folder
.BI user N\fR.
.B auto
does so only when the files come from more than one user area.
.TP
.B \-\-trim
Cut the ^Z padding off the end of a text file (CP/M keeps most files in whole 128-byte records, padded with ^Z).
.TP
.BI \-\-manifest= FILE
Also write a retro-files manifest of what was extracted.
.TP
.BR \-\-replace ", " \-\-skip
What to do with a host file of the same name: replace it, or leave it and go on.
Without either, the run stops with exit status 5.
.RE
.SS put
.B cpmdw put
.I image host... dir
.PP
Add host files and folders to
.IR dir ,
a user area such as
.BR 0: .
Folders called
.B user0
to
.B user15
go to those user areas.
Names are made valid CP/M 8.3 names; a name that clashes within one
.B put
gets
.BR ~1 ,
.BR ~2 ...
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-replace ", " \-\-skip
What to do when a file of that name is on the disc.
Without either, the run stops with exit status 5.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Replace a read-only file too.
.TP
.BI \-\-retro= FILE
Take names, user areas and attributes from a retro-files manifest or JSON file (the drag-and-drop format shared with
Acorn Disk Wrangler and FLEX Disk Wrangler); may be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-\-letters= dirs | flatten
Accepted as the other Disk Wranglers accept it (Acorn DFS files arriving on an ADFS disc); it changes nothing on CP/M.
.RE
.SS rm
.B cpmdw rm
.I image path...
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.PP
Delete files.
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recursive
deletes a whole user area
.RB ( 3: );
.B \-\-force
deletes read-only files too.
.SS mkdir
.B cpmdw mkdir
.I image path...
.RB [ \-p ]
.PP
CP/M has no directories, so this always fails with exit status 6.
.RB ( \-p ", " \-\-parents
is accepted for the other Disk Wranglers' sake.)
.SS mv
.B cpmdw mv
.I image from to
.PP
Rename a file, or move it to another user area
.RB ( "cpmdw mv disc.img 0:A.TXT 3:B.TXT" ).
.SS attr
.B cpmdw attr
.I image path...
.RB [ \-\-set
.IR key = value ]...
.PP
Show a file's attributes, or change them with
.BR \-\-set ,
which may be given more than once.
The keys are
.BR readonly ,
.BR system ,
.B archive
and
.BR f1 \- f4 ;
the values
.BR yes / no ,
.BR on / off ,
.BR true / false
or
.BR 1 / 0 .
.SS title
.B cpmdw title
.I image
.RI [ text ]
.PP
Show or set a CP/M 3 disc's label (a disc made with
.BR "new \-\-stamps" ).
Other CP/M discs have none (exit status 6).
.SS boot
.B cpmdw boot
.I image
.RI [ option ]
.PP
CP/M discs have no boot option: this exists for the other Disk Wranglers and fails with exit status 6.
.SS new
.B cpmdw new
.I file
.BI \-\-format= NAME
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B cpmdw new \-\-list
.PP
Make a blank disc of a format from the library.
The disc is checked and opened again before it is kept.
The new disc's directory is empty, so commands that change it need
.B \-\-format
too (see
.BR Formats ).
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the formats (with
.BR \-\-json ,
each one's description, layout and whether it exists double-sided).
.TP
.BI \-\-container= KIND
.B raw
(the default),
.B imd
(ImageDisk),
.B dsk
(the standard CPCEMU DSK, which older emulators read) or
.B edsk
(extended DSK).
An ImageDisk file records each track's density as the real disc has it: single-density formats are FM, a
double-density disc's single-density track 0 is FM.
.TP
.B \-\-stamps
A CP/M 3 disc with time stamps (the format's layout, with CP/M 3's directory).
.TP
.BI \-\-label= TEXT
The disc label; needs
.BR \-\-stamps .
.TP
.B \-\-separate\-sides
A double-sided disc with a separate CP/M disc on each side, as Research Machines' are, for a format that exists so
.RB ( rm\-sd ,
.BR rm\-dd ,
.BR rm\-qd ).
A raw file holds all of side 0, then all of side 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-track0= own | padded
For a raw file of a format whose track 0 is smaller than the other tracks (a single-density track 0): keep it at its
own size
.RB ( own ,
as real Research Machines images are), or pad it with zeros to a full track
.RB ( padded ,
as some emulators want).
.TP
.B \-\-replace
Replace the file if it exists.
.RE
.SS convert
.B cpmdw convert
.I image
.RI [ out ]
.RB [ \-\-container= \fIKIND\fR]
.PP
Write the disc in another container
.RB ( raw ,
.BR imd ,
.B dsk
or
.BR edsk ),
to
.I out
or over the image.
A raw disc saved as ImageDisk or DSK gets the tracks a new disc of its format has (a single-density track 0 back,
say).
TeleDisk files can't be written, so convert one to change it.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-track0= own | padded
As for
.BR new .
.TP
.B \-\-replace
Replace
.I out
if it exists.
.RE
.SS copy
.B cpmdw copy
.I src dest path...
.RB [ \-\-into= \fIDIR\fR]
.RB [ \-\-to\-vol= \fINAME\fR]
.PP
Copy files from one image to another (or within one), mapping names as drag and drop does.
.B \-\-into
names the user area they go to,
.B \-\-to\-vol
the volume of
.IR dest .
.BR \-\-replace ,
.BR \-\-skip ,
.B \-\-force
and
.B \-\-letters
are as for
.BR put .
.SS script
.B cpmdw script
.I image
.RI [ file | \- ]
.PP
Run many commands on one image with one save.
Each line of
.I file
(or standard input) is a command without
.B cpmdw
and the image
.RB ( "put host/GAME.COM 2:" );
lines starting
.B #
are comments.
Global options go on the
.B script
command itself;
.BR new ,
.B convert
and
.B script
can't be used in a script.
It stops at the first line that fails, and then nothing is saved.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
With
.BR \-\-json ,
standard output is one JSON document (schema 1):
.BR schema ,
.BR tool ,
.BR command ,
.BR image ,
.BR vol ,
.BR ok ,
.BR exit ,
.BR dryRun ,
.BR saved ,
.B result
(which depends on the command),
.B changes
(one per file added, deleted, renamed or changed),
.B warnings
and
.B error
(null, or
.BR code ,
.BR kind ,
.B message
and
.BR path ).
Scripts should read
.B exit
for the outcome and the rest for detail.
Text output is for people and may change between versions; the JSON only gains fields.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 4
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed (the disc is full, a bad name, a file that can't be read).
.TP
.B 2
Wrong arguments.
.TP
.B 3
The image, a path on it, or a host file isn't there.
.TP
.B 4
The disc fails its check, so nothing was written.
.TP
.B 5
A name exists and neither
.B \-\-replace
nor
.B \-\-skip
was given.
.TP
.B 6
Unsupported, or read-only: a TeleDisk file, a disc whose format isn't confirmed (give
.BR \-\-format ),
something the filesystem can't do.
.TP
.B 7
The disc belongs to another Disk Wrangler (an Acorn or FLEX disc;
.B info
names it).
.TP
.B 8
Two readings are equally likely: choose one with
.BR \-\-fs .
.TP
.B 9
Nothing recognised the disc
.RB ( \-\-fs
tries a reading anyway).
.TP
.B 10
The checked save failed; the original file is untouched.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
If set, the time used for CP/M 3 time stamps and ImageDisk headers, so that a run gives the same bytes every time.
.SH EXAMPLES
In a folder holding the files
.I HELLO.TXT
and
.IR GAME.COM :
.PP
.EX
cpmdw new games.img \-\-format=kpii                  # a blank Kaypro II disc
cpmdw put games.img \-\-format=kpii HELLO.TXT GAME.COM 0:
cpmdw ls games.img
cpmdw get games.img 0:GAME.COM out/                # out/game.com
cpmdw check games.img
cpmdw convert games.img games.dsk \-\-container=dsk
cpmdw \-\-dry\-run rm games.img 0:HELLO.TXT
cpmdw new rm.imd \-\-format=rm\-sd \-\-separate\-sides \-\-container=imd
cpmdw \-\-json ls games.img
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cpmdiskwrangler ,
the program with a window;
.BR acorndw (1)
and
.BR flexdw (1),
the other Disk Wranglers' console programs;
.BR cpmls (1),
.BR cpmcp (1)
(cpmtools).
.PP
